<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE topic SYSTEM "https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/xhtml-entities.dtd">
<topic xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
       xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/topic.v2.xsd"
       id="plugin-lifecycle" title="The plugin lifecycle">

    <show-structure for="chapter" depth="2"/>

    <tldr>
        <p>
            Assumes a hand-built weaver — no shipped driver loads a plugin at all.
            <a href="what-a-plugin-can-do.topic">What a plugin can do</a> has why.
        </p>
    </tldr>

    <p>
        A <code>WeaverPlugin</code> is admitted, contributes once, and afterwards only hears what
        the weaver already did. Once sorting has settled the order, each gate decides on its own.
        A plugin that fails one is dropped with a diagnostic, and the rest still get a hearing.
    </p>

    <code-block lang="mermaid">
flowchart TB
    A["Candidates sorted by namespace and class name"] --> B["Identity and namespace"]
    B --> C["Permitted to load"]
    C --> D["SPI generation"]
    D --> E["Namespace claimed"]
    E --> F["contribute is called once"]
    F --> G["Registry frozen"]
    G --> H["Events until the run ends"]
    B --> X1["Dropped: AW3114 or AW3101"]
    C --> X2["Dropped: AW3119"]
    D --> X3["Dropped: AW3112, AW3113 or AW3114"]
    E --> X4["Dropped: AW3111"]
    F --> X5["Dropped: AW3115"]
    </code-block>

    <chapter title="The four gates" id="gates">
        <p>
            Sorting happens before the first gate, and it reads <code>id()</code> directly —
            not through the guard the gates below use. With two or more candidates, a throw
            there ends the whole build with no diagnostic. Classpath order otherwise decides
            nothing; the <a href="determinism.topic">determinism guarantee</a> covers why.
        </p>

        <deflist type="full">
            <def title="Identity and namespace">
                <p>
                    <code>id()</code> is asked for first, and many times afterwards. Return a
                    constant. A throw during a gate is <code>AW3114</code>: take the work out of
                    that method. The empty namespace belongs to the framework, and claiming it is
                    <code>AW3101</code> — pick one of your own.
                    <a href="namespaces.topic">Namespaces and identifiers</a> has the shape.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="Permission">
                <p>
                    A predicate the caller passed in answers whether that namespace may load.
                    Refusal is <code>AW3119</code>: change what the predicate admits, or take the
                    jar off the classpath. Unconfigured, it permits everything.
                    <a href="diagnostics-policy.topic">AW3xxx</a> has the settings a driver could
                    build that predicate from.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="SPI generation">
                <p>
                    <code>apiLevel()</code> answers <code>WeaverApi.LEVEL</code>, and a plugin
                    that fails this gate never runs a line of its own contribution code.
                    <a href="spi.topic">The plugin SPI</a> has the rule and the two codes a
                    mismatch carries.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="The namespace claim">
                <p>
                    A namespace has exactly one owner. The second plugin to claim one is
                    <code>AW3111</code> and contributes nothing.
                    <a href="namespaces.topic">Namespaces and identifiers</a> has the remedy, and
                    why the loser is the same on every machine.
                </p>
            </def>
        </deflist>

        <note>
            <p>
                The engine's own built-in plugin skips two gates, not one. It is exempt from the
                namespace-ownership check and from permission, since the empty namespace is its
                own. Its SPI generation and its namespace claim are still checked, so mismatched
                engine jars are refused rather than half-loaded.
            </p>
        </note>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Contributing, once" id="contribute">
        <p>
            <code>contribute</code> runs once, on the thread building the weaver, and only after
            every gate has passed. What it registers on the context is read off when the call
            returns. <a href="what-a-plugin-can-do.topic">What a plugin can do</a> lists what may
            go there.
        </p>

        <list>
            <li><p>A throw is <code>AW3115</code>, and it costs the plugin everything. Whatever it
                already registered goes too, along with its seat in the audience. Report through
                the context instead of throwing.</p></li>
            <li><p>One bad contribution is cheaper. A factory registering an identifier outside
                its own namespace is dropped as <code>AW3110</code>, and the plugin's other
                factories still stand. <a href="namespaces.topic">Namespaces and identifiers</a>
                has the grammar to prefix it with.</p></li>
            <li><p>A plugin that registers nothing still counts as a contributor. The registry
                goes into the plan's fingerprint, which the
                <a href="pipeline.topic">weaving pipeline</a> reads back.</p></li>
        </list>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="After the registry is frozen" id="events">
        <p>
            Four events reach <code>observe</code>. Each one reports work already finished.
            Nothing an observer returns is read, and no event carries bytes.
        </p>

        <table style="header-row">
            <tr><td>Event</td><td>Arrives</td><td>Goes to</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>PluginsLoaded</code></td><td>once, before planning</td>
                <td>every loaded plugin</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Prepared</code></td><td>after planning, before the first class</td>
                <td>every loaded plugin</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>ClassWoven</code></td><td>per class rewritten and stamped</td>
                <td>plugins that opted in</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>WeavingFinished</code></td><td>when a driver ends the run</td>
                <td>every loaded plugin</td></tr>
        </table>

        <list>
            <li><p>The first two arrive on the thread that built the weaver.
                <code>ClassWoven</code> arrives on the thread that wove the class, and
                <a href="load-time-weaving.topic">load-time weaving</a> uses several at once. An
                observer holding state needs it thread-safe.</p></li>
            <li><p>A run does not always end: only the Maven goal calls <code>finish()</code>.
                The agent, a weaving class loader and the testkit never do, so
                <code>WeavingFinished</code> is not promised there.
                <a href="choose-a-driver.topic">Choose a driver</a> compares all four.</p></li>
            <li><p>An observer that throws is <code>AW3118</code>, a warning. The remaining
                plugins still hear the event. Fix the observer; nothing was miswoven.</p></li>
        </list>

        <p>
            Contributed factories are called far later too, and the two calls are not contained
            alike. A contributed point that throws is <code>AW3116</code>. It costs everything
            that point matched in that method — the whole declaration, since it was the only
            point. A declaration with a <code>require</code> minimum also fails accounting, as
            <code>AW1043</code>, and the class is left unmodified.
        </p>

        <p>
            Emitting the bytes of a class wraps the whole rewrite instead. A throw there can cost
            weaves that were never the plugin's.
            <a href="diagnostics-policy.topic">AW3xxx</a> has both codes.
        </p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Which run loads a plugin at all" id="who">
        <p>
            Both routes into the loader belong to whoever builds the weaver. One installs an
            instance directly. The other searches a class loader the caller names. The Maven goal,
            the agent and the weaving class loader ask for neither. Their runs reach the registry
            with the built-in plugin alone.
            <a href="what-a-plugin-can-do.topic">What a plugin can do</a> states the boundary
            behind that.
        </p>
    </chapter>

